    "generated_by": "ai",
    "solution": "Write $u=x+\\sqrt{x^{2}+1}.$ Then $u'=1+\\frac{x}{\\sqrt{x^{2}+1}}=\\frac{\\sqrt{x^{2}+1}+x}{\\sqrt{x^{2}+1}},$ so by the chain rule $\\frac{d}{dx}\\ln u=\\frac{u'}{u}=\\frac{\\sqrt{x^{2}+1}+x}{\\sqrt{x^{2}+1}}\\cdot\\frac{1}{x+\\sqrt{x^{2}+1}}=\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{1+x^{2}}}.$ Since $u>0$ for every $x,$ the formula holds on the whole real line."
