   "prompt": "<p>For the following exercises, verify the derivatives and antiderivatives.</p><p>$\\frac{d}{dx}\\ln(\\frac{1+\\sqrt{1-x^{2}}}{x})=-\\frac{1}{x\\sqrt{1-x^{2}}}$</p>",
   "answer": {
    "type": "open",
    "generated_by": "ai",
    "solution": "For $0<x<1,$ write $\\ln\\left(\\frac{1+\\sqrt{1-x^{2}}}{x}\\right)=\\ln(1+\\sqrt{1-x^{2}})-\\ln x.$ The derivative of the first term is $\\frac{1}{1+\\sqrt{1-x^{2}}}\\cdot\\frac{-x}{\\sqrt{1-x^{2}}},$ and of the second $\\frac{1}{x}.$ Over the common denominator $x\\sqrt{1-x^{2}}\\,(1+\\sqrt{1-x^{2}}),$ the difference has numerator $-x^{2}-\\sqrt{1-x^{2}}(1+\\sqrt{1-x^{2}})=-x^{2}-\\sqrt{1-x^{2}}-(1-x^{2})=-(1+\\sqrt{1-x^{2}}),$ so the derivative is $-\\frac{1}{x\\sqrt{1-x^{2}}}.$"
