    "generated_by": "ai",
    "solution": "Differentiate the right side. With $u=\\ln(\\ln x),$ $\\frac{d}{dx}\\ln u=\\frac{u'}{u},$ and $u'=\\frac{1}{\\ln x}\\cdot\\frac{1}{x}.$ So $\\frac{d}{dx}\\left[\\ln(\\ln(\\ln x))+C\\right]=\\frac{1}{\\ln(\\ln x)}\\cdot\\frac{1}{x\\ln x}=\\frac{1}{x\\ln(x)\\ln(\\ln x)},$ the integrand, for $x>e,$ where $\\ln(\\ln x)>0.$ Equivalently, the substitution $w=\\ln(\\ln x),$ $dw=\\frac{dx}{x\\ln x},$ turns the integral into $\\int\\frac{dw}{w}=\\ln|w|+C.$"
   }
